/F1 13 Tf
(ABSTRACT) Tj
/F1 11 Tf
1 0 0 1 50 638 Tm
() Tj
1 0 0 1 50 622 Tm
(The proliferation of Internet of Things devices has introduced unprecedented security) Tj
1 0 0 1 50 606 Tm
(challenges in modern network infrastructures. This paper proposes a federated) Tj
1 0 0 1 50 590 Tm
(learning-based anomaly detection framework for identifying malicious traffic patterns in) Tj
1 0 0 1 50 574 Tm
(heterogeneous IoT environments. Our approach enables distributed model training across) Tj
1 0 0 1 50 558 Tm
(edge nodes without centralizing sensitive network data, thereby preserving privacy while) Tj
1 0 0 1 50 542 Tm
(maintaining detection accuracy. Experimental evaluation on the CIC-IoT-2023 and N-BaIoT) Tj
1 0 0 1 50 526 Tm
(benchmark datasets demonstrates that our federated approach achieves 96.2 percent) Tj
1 0 0 1 50 510 Tm
(detection accuracy with a false positive rate below 1.8 percent, comparable to centralized) Tj
1 0 0 1 50 494 Tm
(baselines while reducing data transmission overhead by 78 percent.) Tj
1 0 0 1 50 478 Tm
() Tj
1 0 0 1 50 462 Tm
/F1 13 Tf
(Keywords: IoT Security, Federated Learning, Anomaly Detection, Network Intrusion Detection, Edge Computing, Privacy) Tj
/F1 11 Tf
1 0 0 1 50 446 Tm
() Tj
1 0 0 1 50 430 Tm
/F1 13 Tf
(1. INTRODUCTION) Tj
/F1 11 Tf
1 0 0 1 50 414 Tm
(The rapid expansion of Internet of Things ecosystems has created vast attack surfaces) Tj
1 0 0 1 50 398 Tm
(that traditional perimeter-based security models cannot adequately protect. By 2025, an) Tj
1 0 0 1 50 382 Tm
(estimated 75 billion IoT devices are projected to be deployed globally, generating massive) Tj
1 0 0 1 50 366 Tm
(volumes of heterogeneous network traffic. Centralized monitoring approaches face) Tj
1 0 0 1 50 350 Tm
(scalability limitations and raise significant privacy concerns when processing data from) Tj
1 0 0 1 50 334 Tm
(distributed sensor networks. Federated learning offers a promising paradigm for) Tj
1 0 0 1 50 318 Tm
(collaborative model training that keeps raw data at the network edge.) Tj
1 0 0 1 50 302 Tm
() Tj
1 0 0 1 50 286 Tm
/F1 13 Tf
(2. SYSTEM ARCHITECTURE) Tj
/F1 11 Tf
1 0 0 1 50 270 Tm
(Our proposed framework consists of three principal components: \(1\) lightweight traffic) Tj
1 0 0 1 50 254 Tm
(feature extractors deployed on IoT gateway nodes, \(2\) local anomaly detection models) Tj
1 0 0 1 50 238 Tm
(trained using gradient-based federated optimization, and \(3\) a central aggregation server) Tj
1 0 0 1 50 222 Tm
(that coordinates model updates using secure aggregation protocols. Each edge node) Tj
1 0 0 1 50 206 Tm
(maintains a local Random Forest and LSTM hybrid model that processes network flow) Tj
1 0 0 1 50 190 Tm
(statistics including packet rates, byte distributions, and protocol entropy measurements.) Tj
1 0 0 1 50 174 Tm
() Tj
1 0 0 1 50 158 Tm
/F1 13 Tf
(3. EXPERIMENTAL EVALUATION) Tj
/F1 11 Tf
1 0 0 1 50 142 Tm
(We evaluate our framework using two established IoT security benchmarks. The CIC-IoT-2023) Tj
1 0 0 1 50 126 Tm
(dataset contains 33 attack categories across smart home, industrial, and healthcare IoT) Tj
1 0 0 1 50 110 Tm
(scenarios. The N-BaIoT dataset comprises traffic from nine commercial IoT devices under) Tj
1 0 0 1 50 94 Tm
(both benign and botnet-infected conditions. Our federated approach achieves an F1-score of) Tj
1 0 0 1 50 78 Tm
/F1 13 Tf
(0.954 on CIC-IoT-2023 and 0.968 on N-BaIoT, with convergence achieved within 15) Tj
/F1 11 Tf
1 0 0 1 50 62 Tm
(communication rounds.) Tj
